---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import InterestForm from "../components/InterestForm.astro";
---

<Base
  title="Andorra self-employed residence (J.1)"
  description="The Government’s J.1 self-employed residence-and-work page: quota of 200 under Decret 75/2026, two published tracks, documents."
  path="/self-employed-residence"
>
  <section class="page-header">
    <h1>Self-employed residence and work (J.1)</h1>
    <p>
      The Government still publishes a dedicated J.1 page for newcomers
      who will work on their own account. This is that page.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="prose">
    <div class="notice">
      <p>
        Source:
        <a
          href="https://www.govern.ad/ca/tematiques/immigracio/residencia-i-treball/residencia-i-treball-per-compte-propi-autoritzacio-inicial"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener"
          >J.1. Residència i treball per compte propi: autorització inicial
          — Govern d’Andorra</a
        >.
        Renewal is J.2 on the same official index. Law 2/2026 recast the
        former AFA deposit, for several self-employed cases, as a
        non-refundable income. The live J.1 page still names 50,000 euros
        at filing. Official publications also describe a
        <a
          href="https://www.govern.ad/ca/tematiques/immigracio/residencia-i-treball/reserva-autoritzacio-de-residencia-i-treball-per-compte-propi"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener">J reservation</a
        >
        and a 2026 quota regulation. If the texts disagree, use the BOPA
        and the quota decree.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2>What the Government says J.1 is</h2>
    <p>
      J.1 authorises the holder to reside and to exercise a self-employed
      activity on a permanent basis for the life of the authorisation. A
      newcomer who comes within the quota then set by regulation may
      apply, under current law.
    </p>
    <p>
      A
      <a
        href="https://www.govern.ad/ca/w/aprovada-una-nova-quota-d-autoritzacions-d-immigracio-de-residencia-i-treball-per-compte-propi-i-de-treball-sense-residencia"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener">Govern news note</a
      >
      describes Decret 75/2026 setting a general self-employed quota of
      200 authorisations: 150 for foreign-investment cases, 50 for
      liberal professions. Confirm whether a place is actually being
      issued.
    </p>

    <h2>How long the official page says it lasts</h2>
    <p>
      The first authorisation is for one year, renewable three times for
      periods of two years. After seven years, later renewals are for ten
      years, except for nationals of states that have signed and ratified
      a convention with Andorra (the page points to J.2).
    </p>

    <h2>Two tracks the page names</h2>
    <h3>Foreign investment</h3>
    <p>The additional papers on the official list are:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        the corresponding foreign-investment authorisation to constitute
        an Andorran company in which the person holds more than 34%, and
        legal constitution of that company;
      </li>
      <li>
        proof, within three months of the immigration application, that
        the company holds a commerce that is duly registered and active
        in Andorra;
      </li>
      <li>
        an unremunerated deposit with the Andorran Financial Authority,
        paid at the same time as the immigration application. The euro
        figure is on the live page.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Liberal profession</h3>
    <p>
      Within three months of the immigration application, the person must
      show that they have obtained the Government’s authorisation to
      practise the liberal profession and, where it applies, membership
      of the relevant professional college.
    </p>

    <h2>Documents listed (non-exhaustive)</h2>
    <p>
      The official list is expressly “enunciative and not limiting”. It
      includes, among other items:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>the completed self-employed residence-and-work application;</li>
      <li>passport, or for EU/EEA nationals a national identity card;</li>
      <li>
        criminal-record certificates from the country of origin, of
        nationality and of residence;
      </li>
      <li>a recent colour photograph to the stated format;</li>
      <li>
        proof of accommodation (lease, title, or a public-establishment
        certificate; a domicile certificate if the dwelling is not in
        the applicant’s name);
      </li>
      <li>civil-status documents as listed on the page;</li>
      <li>
        a curriculum vitae with originals and copies of diplomas or
        other proof of professional qualification.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Foreign official documents must carry a Hague apostille or be
      otherwise legalised. After a grant, the page requires registration
      with the Comú of the parish of residence within three months, and
      proof of that registration within the same period.
    </p>

    <h2>Renewals and activity</h2>
    <p>
      Official renewal papers ask for documents proving the company’s
      commercial activity in the period being renewed. They do not set a
      turnover figure. Related:
      <a href="/become-a-resident">permit types</a>,
      <a href="/residence-without-work">D.1</a>,
      <a href="/entrepreneur-residence">D.4</a>,
      <a href="/buying-property">buying as a non-resident</a>,
      <a href="/opening-a-bank-account">banks</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <InterestForm
    intro="Leave an email if you are looking at J.1 and want an introduction to counsel."
    source="self-employed-residence"
  />
</Base>
